PLACES=[
N('rookery-fort-leavenworth-ks','The Rookery (Building 19), Fort Leavenworth','Fort Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.35495,-94.91700,'site','permit','drive','','1828–1878',
  'The oldest surviving building at Fort Leavenworth, built as the post commander\'s quarters and office. The Historic American Buildings Survey says its southern part was built between 1828 and 1832, with a second storey and remodelling about 1870 to 1875 and a northern section planned in 1878. Colonel Henry Dodge and Colonel Stephen Kearny lived here in the 1830s, and Andrew Reeder, the first governor of Kansas Territory, lived here in 1854. Dates differ by source: the Wikipedia article says it was built between 1832 and 1834, and the City of Leavenworth\'s visitor page says about 1834.',
  'A two-storey limestone house on a high basement, with two tiers of verandahs that the survey says carry traces of French Colonial building. The walls are stone quarried on the post. It stands at the northeast corner of the Main Parade. The Army says it is still lived in, and the survey found it divided into two homes for colonels\' families, so there is no way inside. Interior not checked.',
  'The oldest building in Kansas, still lived in.',
  FTACC+' The Rookery is a family home: look from the parade ground.',
  [['Historic American Buildings Survey: Building 19, the Rookery',HABS19],['Army leaflet: Fort Leavenworth wayside tour (stop 18)',WAY],['City of Leavenworth: the Rookery','https://www.leavenworthks.gov/visitors/page/rookery'],W('Fort Leavenworth')],brick='no'),
N('arsenal-halls-fort-leavenworth-ks','Sherman, Sheridan and Grant Halls (the Arsenal), Fort Leavenworth','Fort Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.35135,-94.91495,'site','permit','drive','','1859–1916',
  'The Army\'s wayside leaflet says Sherman, Sheridan, Grant and Wagner Halls were built between 1859 and 1916 as part of the Fort Leavenworth Arsenal, and that Sherman and Sheridan were first ordnance storehouses. Wikipedia names Sherman and Sheridan as the two surviving buildings of the arsenal. The Historic American Buildings Survey says the group centred on Grant Hall served from 1890 to 1959 as headquarters of the Army\'s cavalry and infantry school and later of the Command and General Staff College.',
  'A cluster of large buildings round Grant Hall at the end of Scott Avenue, one of the best known sights on the post. The pin is between Grant Hall and Sherman Hall. Offices of the Combined Arms Center are here today. Interior not checked.',
  'The arsenal storehouses that became the Army\'s staff college.',
  FTACC+' The halls are working offices.',
  [['Army leaflet: Fort Leavenworth wayside tour (stop 9)',WAY],['Historic American Buildings Survey: Fort Leavenworth overview',HABSFL],W('Fort Leavenworth')],brick='?'),
N('memorial-chapel-fort-leavenworth-ks','Memorial Chapel, Fort Leavenworth','Fort Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',39.35508,-94.91636,'exact','permit','drive','','1878',
  'The post\'s first Protestant chapel, built in 1878 by prison labour of stone quarried on the post, according to Wikipedia. The Historic American Buildings Survey calls it the only real revival of the Gothic style on the post and one of only a few stone buildings still standing there.',
  'A small Gothic stone church on Scott Avenue. Wikipedia says brass cannon are set into the side walls and that the round window behind the altar was fitted slightly askew by an inmate; neither is confirmed by the survey. Whether visitors may go in, and when, was not found: interior not checked.',
  'A stone Gothic chapel built by prisoners in 1878.',
  FTACC+' Service times were not found on a page read for this entry.',
  [['Wikipedia: Fort Leavenworth (the chapel)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Leavenworth'],['Historic American Buildings Survey: Fort Leavenworth overview',HABSFL]],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('fort-leavenworth-national-cemetery-ks','Fort Leavenworth National Cemetery','Fort Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','burial','standing',39.35139,-94.93056,'site','permit','drive','','1844–',
  'Graves were dug here from about 1844 and the burial ground was made a national cemetery in 1862. Sources disagree on how many came first: the Army\'s leaflet calls it one of the first 14 national cemeteries set up under Abraham Lincoln, and Wikipedia calls it one of the twelve original ones. The leaflet gives more than 23,000 graves from every conflict since the War of 1812.',
  'Rows of headstones on the western side of the post. The pin is the point Wikipedia and the National Register give, not checked on the aerial for the cemetery gate. Stop 22 on the Army\'s self-guided tour.',
  '',
  FTACC+' The cemetery\'s own page could not be opened, so its hours were not checked.',
  [['Army leaflet: Fort Leavenworth wayside tour (stop 22)',WAY],W('Fort Leavenworth National Cemetery')],brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('buffalo-soldier-monument-fort-leavenworth-ks','Buffalo Soldier Monument, Fort Leavenworth','Fort Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','curio','marker',39.34597,-94.91924,'exact','permit','drive','','1992',
  'A bronze statue of a mounted cavalry soldier honouring the African American soldiers of the Buffalo Soldier regiments formed in 1866. General Colin Powell dedicated it in 1992 (Army leaflet).',
  'A statue of a cavalry rider on Grant Avenue, in the grounds near the Frontier Army Museum.',
  '',
  FTACC+' The Frontier Army Museum at 100 Reynolds Avenue starts the self-guided tour; its hours were not checked.',
  [['Army leaflet: Fort Leavenworth wayside tour (stop 7)',WAY],W('Fort Leavenworth')],brick='no'),
N('old-kansas-state-penitentiary-lansing-ks','Old Kansas State Penitentiary (Lansing Historical Society and Museum)','Lansing, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',39.2511,-94.8936,'site','open','drive','','1860s–2020',
  'The state prison at Lansing took Kansas convicts from July 1868 and was built by prison labour in the 1860s. The Kansas Territorial Legislature set it up in 1859, according to the City of Leavenworth\'s page. It also held Oklahoma felons from 1889 to 1909. Perry Smith and Richard Hickock, convicted of the 1959 Clutter killings, were hanged here in 1965. A new main prison opened in 2020 and the Lansing Historical Society took the old one over in September 2024.',
  'Limestone cellhouses and walls, quarried and laid by the early inmates (City of Leavenworth). Guided "Behind the Walls" tours go through the cellhouses and the hall where Johnny and June Carter Cash performed. Each tour starts at the Lansing Historical Society and Museum.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Lansing Historical Society. Tours are by ticket, $30 a person, and the society advises booking ahead; nobody under 12 is let inside the walls. Park at the Lansing Historical Society and Museum on the prison entrance road. The pin is the prison on Wikipedia\'s coordinate, not checked against the museum door.',
  [['Lansing Historical Society: tour tickets and times','https://lansingkansashistory.com/ksp-tours/'],['Kansas Department of Corrections: keys handed to the society, 2024','https://www.doc.ks.gov/kdoc-turns-keys-to-old-kansas-state-penitentiary-lcf-to-lhs'],['City of Leavenworth visitors: former Kansas State Penitentiary tours','https://www.leavenworthks.gov/visitors/page/former-kansas-state-penitentiary-tours'],W('Lansing Correctional Facility')],brick='no',hours='Guided tours Friday, Saturday and Sunday at 9 a.m., noon and 3 p.m. (as read November 2026; the page gives no season)'),
N('leavenworth-industrial-district-ks','Leavenworth Historic Industrial District and the Great Western Stove works','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','industry','standing',39.3140,-94.9100,'area','view','drive','','1860–1934',
  'A group of brick factory buildings between South Second and Third streets and Three Mile Creek, which the City of Leavenworth calls a rare intact 19th-century industrial and warehouse district. Its page lists the Great Western Stove Company works at 401 to 419 South Second Street (four storeys, 1882, with additions of 1887, 1901 and 1934, used for tyre making until 2004 and then made into lofts), a second stove-works building of 1874 at 402 to 410 South Second, and the Great Western Manufacturing foundry of about 1899 on South Third, which closed in 1988 after making titanium parts for the F-111 fighter. The district is on the National Register (2002).',
  'Four-storey brick factory blocks with stone foundations, double-hipped roofs with clerestories, arched windows with cast-iron sills, and a brick alley between Choctaw and Cherokee streets that keeps its old paving. The pin is the middle of the district, not one building.',
  '',
  'Private and public ownership mixed; the stove works are apartments. Look from the streets. Park on South Second or Third Street.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: Historical Industrial District',LC%'historical-industrial-district'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings','https://www.kansashistory.gov/natreg/natreg_listings/search/page:1/county:LV/records:all/submit:SEARCH']],brick='yes'),
N('north-esplanade-leavenworth-ks','North Esplanade Historic District and Park, Leavenworth','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','town','standing',39.3222,-94.9100,'site','public','walk','','1854–1890s',
  'The first historic district named in Leavenworth (December 12, 1977): fourteen houses along four blocks of North Esplanade, between Ottawa and Seneca streets, facing a park on the bluff over the Missouri River. The city says the park is the oldest public park in Kansas, part of the town plat of December 1854. Larger houses went up from the 1860s to the 1880s, many for managers of local businesses.',
  'A line of houses all built before 1900 and set back the same distance from the street, with a grassy bluff-top park, benches and old-style lamp posts, and two audio posts on Bleeding Kansas and the Anthony family.',
  '',
  'The park is public ground; the houses are private, so look from the park and the sidewalk. Park on North Esplanade Street.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: North Esplanade Historic District',LC%'north-esplanade-historic-district']],brick='some'),
N('arch-street-district-leavenworth-ks','Arch Street Historic District, Leavenworth','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','town','standing',39.30944,-94.90917,'site','view','walk','','1860s–1920s',
  'A ten-acre neighbourhood of houses in Fackler\'s Addition, platted in 1857. The city says the earliest are Italianate houses of the late 1860s and 1870s, with Gothic Revival, Queen Anne and later Classic Revival, Tudor and Craftsman houses. Managers of the Great Western stove and manufacturing companies lived here. The district was listed on the National Register in 2002.',
  'Streets and house lots unchanged since the 1857 plat. The city\'s page names the Wilson House at 221 Arch Street, a work of the Kansas City firm Wright & Wright, and the Fackler house at 222 Vine Street, on the hill.',
  '',
  'Private houses along public streets: look from the sidewalk. Park on Arch Street.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: Arch Street Historic District',LC%'arch-street-historic-district']],brick='some'),
N('third-avenue-district-leavenworth-ks','Third Avenue Historic District, Leavenworth','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','town','standing',39.3075,-94.9147,'site','view','walk','','1859–1920',
  'A neighbourhood platted in 1856 as Day\'s Subdivision. Its two-storey brick and weatherboard houses, mostly Victorian and Queen Anne, were built between 1859 and 1920, and two former schools stand in it. The city\'s page describes the Bishop Miege House at 1028 Second Avenue, a house of the 1860s with Greek Revival and Italianate touches. It was listed in 2002.',
  'Porched two-storey houses with brick or weatherboard walls; some have 1920s and 1930s Craftsman updates. The pin is the middle of the district.',
  '',
  'Private houses: look from the sidewalk.',
  [['City of Leavenworth: Third Avenue Historic District',LC%'third-avenue-historic-district']],brick='some'),
N('union-block-oskaloosa-ks','Union Block, Oskaloosa','Oskaloosa, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.21528,-95.31306,'exact','view','drive','','1892',
  'A row of four two-storey shops and offices on the courthouse square, built in 1892 for local businessmen to a design by H. M. Hadley of Topeka. The east end was built for the State Bank of Oskaloosa. The four fronts cost an estimated $18,500 in all. It was listed on the National Register in 1973.',
  'Red brick in running bond with rough limestone trim and a stone base. The bank corner has an angled entrance between two red granite columns under a limestone arch, and a pediment reads "UNION BLOCK 1892". The 1973 form found the upper storey unchanged and some ground-floor fronts altered. Shops and a restaurant used it then; what is inside now was not checked.',
  '',
  'Private shops on the square; walk in as a customer where one is open. Park on the square. Hours not checked.',
  [['National Register form, Union Block (73000760)',NR%'73000760'],W('Union Block (Oskaloosa, Kansas)')],brick='yes'),
N('old-jefferson-town-oskaloosa-ks','Old Jefferson Town (Jefferson County Historical Society), Oskaloosa','Oskaloosa, Kansas','ks','town','standing',39.21318,-95.30945,'site','open','drive','','1880s–1970s',
  'A village of moved old buildings on about ten acres, begun in 1967 by the Jefferson County Historical Society to show the county\'s built past. It holds a general store, a chapel built near McLouth in 1891, a one-room school of 1887 (the Wellman Schoolhouse), a blacksmith shop, a jail and a Victorian house (the Nincehelser House), plus the boyhood home of the painter John Steuart Curry. The iron bowstring bridge on the grounds is on the National Register (1990).',
  'Old frame and stone buildings along a gravel lane, the Curry home as a small museum, and a genealogy library. The pin is the middle of the grounds.',
  '',
  'Run by the Jefferson County Historical Society at 703 Walnut Street. Hours were not found on the society\'s own page: a directory lists weekend afternoons in summer, which was not confirmed. Telephone 785 863 2070 (from a directory, not the society\'s page) before going.',
  [['Clio: Old Jefferson Town and the Curry boyhood home','https://www.theclio.com/entry/12348'],['Kansas Historical Society: Jefferson County register listings',KSJ2]],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('newell-johnson-searle-house-oskaloosa-ks','Newell-Johnson-Searle House, Oskaloosa','Oskaloosa, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.21467,-95.31067,'site','view','drive','','1858–1918',
  'A house tied to the founding of Oskaloosa. Newell, a co-founder of the town and a figure of the Bleeding Kansas years, owned it first; banker Francis Johnson lived here from 1874 to 1905 and repaired the run-down frame house in 1877; his grandson, the stockman Francis Searle, lived here from 1905 to 1918 and rebuilt much of it after a fire in 1913. It was listed in 2017.',
  'A frame house on Walnut Street whose present look comes from Searle\'s rebuilding. Inside not checked.',
  '',
  'At 609 Walnut Street, a private house as far as the listing shows; look from the street.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society: Jefferson County register listings',KSJ]],brick='?'),
N('abernathy-plant-k-leavenworth-ks','Abernathy Furniture Company Plant K','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','industry','standing',39.32864,-94.91212,'site','view','drive','','1926',
  'Plant K was built in 1926 half a mile north of the Abernathy Furniture Company\'s original factory, because the old site at North Second and Seneca streets was hemmed in. The 2016 National Register nomination says it let the firm double its output at a time when many Leavenworth furniture makers were closing, and that Abernathy was one of the longest-running industries in town and one of the largest furniture makers in the region. The company began in 1883.',
  'Four brick buildings of 1926 between one and four storeys: the four-storey Factory Building, a two-storey Engine and Electrical Building, a one-storey Lumber Kiln and a one-storey Lumber Building, joined by later metal and brick additions. The form says the Engine and Electrical Building has not been renovated and keeps its old inside and outside look. The rail sidings that served it are gone. It stands at North Second Street and Metropolitan Avenue beside the Missouri River.',
  '',
  'At 1100 North 2nd Street. Who owns it and what it is used for now were not checked. Look from the public streets.',
  [['National Register form, Abernathy Furniture Company Factory, Plant K (16000131)',NR%'16000131'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings','https://www.kansashistory.gov/natreg/natreg_listings/search/page:1/county:LV/records:all/submit:SEARCH']],brick='yes'),
]
